        <choice correct="yes">
          <cline>    message = "Hi"</cline>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <cline>    message == "Hi" </cline>
        </choice>
      </block>
    </blocks>
  </activity>
    

    <p>Click on next to trace the execution of nested conditional statements.</p>
 <activity label="codelensNestedIfs">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Keep clicking on the Next button at the bottom of the code to see how
          the values of the variables change as you step through the running
          program. You can see interactive tracing in any Active Code exercise by clicking the CodeLens button. 
        </p>
      </statement>

      <program interactive="codelens" language="python" label="codeLensNestedIfs1" xml:id="codelensNested1">
        <code>
grade = 50
if grade &gt;= 90:
    print("A")
elif grade &gt;= 80:
    print("B")
elif grade &gt;= 70:
    print("C")
elif grade &gt;= 60:
    print("D")
else:
    print("F")
        </code>
      </program>
    </activity>
  
   <activity label="trace-nested-ifs">
      <statement>
        <p>What does the following code print when x has been set to 5 and y is set to -10?</p>
        <program language="java">
          <code>
if x &gt; 0 and y &gt; 0:
    print("positive")
elif x &gt; 0 or y &gt; 0:
    print("positive1")
else:
    print("negative1")
          </code>
        </program>
      </statement>

       <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>negative1</p>
          </statement>

          <feedback>
            <p>
              Not correct.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>positive</p>
          </statement>

          <feedback>
            <p>This will only print positive if both x <em>and</em> y are positive.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
         <choice>
          <statement>
            <p>Nothing</p>
          </statement>

          <feedback>
            <p>No, one of the conditions is true, so something will be printed.</p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p>positive1</p>
          </statement>

          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct. Since x is positive, but y is negative, the <c>or</c> condition is true 
              since at least 1 of them is positive, so positive1 will be printed.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </activity>
</subsection>
 
 <subsection xml:id="adventure-project">
    <title>Adventure Coding Challenge</title>

    <idx><h>coding challenge</h><h>adventure game</h></idx>
    <idx><h>projects</h><h>adventure game</h></idx>
    
    <project label="coding-challenge-adventure" xml:id="adventure">

      <statement>
          <p>
             Create an adventure game with nested if-else statements. 
             You could work in pairs! First, spend some time planning and designing
             your adventure locations. You may want to create a flowchart or other
             design documentation!
             Fill in the ...'s in the print statements and add more if statements 
             for different choices in the adventure. Be careful with the
             nested indentation! Be creative!
        </p>
      </statement>
      <program interactive="activecode" language="python">
        <code>
    action = input ("You are in ....  What direction do you want go (n,s,e,w)?")
    if action == "n":
        action = input ("You go north and see a .... What do you want to do? Type: run or stay.")
        if action == "run":
            print("You choose to run.")
            print("...")
        else:
            print("You choose to stay.")
            print("...")
    # First level (n,s,e,w) choices
    elif action == "s":
        action = input ("You go south and see a .... What do you want to do?")
        # continue the game with another if/else statement here

    # Add more elif statements for e and w directions here

    # Add a default else for not a valid direction
        

    print("End of Game. Click on Run to try the adventure again.")  
        </code>
        <tests>
          from unittest.gui import TestCaseGui

          class myTests(TestCaseGui):
            def test1(self):
              # remove comments
              actual = "\n".join(
                       line for line in self.getEditorText().splitlines()
                      if line.strip() and not line.lstrip().startswith("#")
                      )
              self.assertNotIn("...",actual,"Replace ... with your own adventure text")
              input_count = actual.count("elif")
              self.assertLessEqual(3,input_count, "at least 3 elif statements")
              input_count = actual.count("else:")
              self.assertLessEqual(3,input_count, "at least 3 else statements")

          myTests().main()
        </tests>
      </program>
    </project>

    <activity label="selection-algorithm-reflection">
      <title>Project Reflection on Algorithm</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Identify the first selection statement that you wrote on your own in your project. 
          Explain how it works:
          <ol>
            <li>What is the condition or Boolean expression being tested?</li>
            <li>When does the condition evaluate to true? </li>
            <li>When does it evaluate to false? </li>
            <li>What happens when if the condition is true? </li>
            <li>What happens when if it is false?</li>
          </ol>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response/>
    </activity>

       <activity label="selection-error-reflection">
      <title>Project Reflection on Errors</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Describe a logic error (especially in the condition of the if statements) that you made or 
           another programmer could make in your project code above.
           Describe the program behavior resulting from this error. 

        </p>
      </statement>
      <response/>
    </activity>

    <idx><h>AI use</h><h>generate test cases</h></idx>

    <activity label="selection-testing-reflection">
      <title>Project Reflection on Testing</title>
      <statement>
        <p>Explain how you tested your code with different inputs to make sure it worked correctly.
        If you have access to a Gen AI system, ask it what test cases it would recommend for your code 
        and describe your prompt and its response.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <response/>
    </activity>
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="vocab-2-3">
    <title>Vocabulary Review</title>

    <activity label="vocab-selection">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Review the vocabulary in this lesson. Drag the vocabulary term from the left and drop it
          on its correct definition on the right.
          Click the "Check Me" button to see if you are correct.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <feedback>
        <p>Find the words in the lesson above and try again.</p>
      </feedback>

      <cardsort>
        <match>
        <premise>selection</premise>
        <response>the process of choosing which statements to execute based on certain conditions</response>
        </match>
        <match>
        <premise>conditional statement</premise>
        <response>a statement that checks a condition and executes different code depending on whether the condition is true or false</response>
        </match>
        <match>
        <premise>else</premise>
        <response>a keyword used in conditional statements to specify code that should be executed if the condition is false</response>
        </match>
        <match>
        <premise>nested conditionals</premise>
        <response>conditional statements that are placed inside other conditional statements</response>
        </match>
      </cardsort>
    </activity>
    </subsection>

